# CONFIG_VIDEO / CONFIG_VIDEO_SHENG_MDSS: re-enabled to visually confirm
# the DSI-bypass test patch (sheng_mdss_dsi_test_patch) with U-Boot's
# OWN backlight enable (GPIO 128 + corrected I2C BL_EN/BL_CFG2/BL_DIMMING
# writes, entirely within sheng_mdss_probe(), no dependency on Linux's
# own driver at all). SPEC.md task #5 log has the full story: disabling
# these confirmed Linux's OWN backlight only works when U-Boot doesn't
# touch GDSC/DISPCC/DSI/RSC first -- but that's specifically about
# LINUX's later re-init, not necessarily about whether OUR OWN direct
# GPIO+I2C backlight control works while these pokes are active. This
# build tests that directly.
# Isolation test result: with CONFIG_VIDEO/SHENG_MDSS off, board.c's
# backlight bring-up is now PROVEN correct via readback (BL_EN reads
# back 0x5f on both chips over real I2C, GPIO128's io_reg reads back
# 0x3 -- driven high AND input pin genuinely high) -- yet still no
# visible light. Conclusion: the LED driver chip really is outputting
# current, but the DSI panel itself was never sent exit_sleep/
# display_on in this build (CONFIG_VIDEO off => sheng_mdss_probe()
# never runs => no panel init at all), so the LCD glass is almost
# certainly still sitting in its default-closed/blank state, physically
# blocking backlight light from getting through regardless of the LEDs
# being lit.
#
# GROUND-TRUTH TEST (SPEC.md task #5 log): user confirmed this exact
# NixOS rootfs DID show a login prompt on screen before, with an
# earlier boot.img -- meaning Linux's own complete driver stack CAN
# drive this panel successfully. With CONFIG_VIDEO_SHENG_MDSS active
# (all our GDSC/DISPCC/DSI/DPU register pokes), Linux's fbdev path now
# fails with "framebuffer is not in virtual address space" (a GEM/vmap
# failure) -- almost certainly our own hardware pokes leaving IOMMU/
# SMMU or DPU state Linux's later re-init can't cleanly recover from,
# the same class of problem as the backlight disruption, just
# manifesting differently. Disabling entirely to test whether Linux's
# OWN complete init succeeds again with sheng_mdss_probe() never
# running at all -- the cleanest possible isolation.
#
# Ground truth confirmed (SPEC.md task #5 log): with this off, Linux
# shows a real login prompt -- hardware is fully capable. Re-enabling
# now that MDP_CLK is back to the real 514MHz rate (see
# sheng_mdss_hw.zig's MDP_CLK_DIV_REG_VAL comment) combined with the
# pclk0/byte0/esc0 clock fix and the VID_CFG0/lane/timing register fix
# -- none of these three fixes have ever been tested together before.
# RESULT: still broken on both sides -- our own pixel push shows
# nothing, and Linux's fbdev vmap failure reproduces identically at
# 514MHz too, same as at the lowered SVS rate. No SMMU/IOMMU fault
# messages in dmesg either (ruling out a translation-fault theory).
# The clock-rate mismatch was a real, worthwhile fix in isolation but
# not the actual disruptor -- whatever sheng_mdss_probe() does still
# corrupts state Linux can't recover from, independent of clock rate.
# Reverting to disabled: this is the one config confirmed to actually
# work (Linux shows a real login prompt). Don't regress past this
# known-good point; resume investigation from here next time.
#
# REAL LIKELY ANSWER FOUND (SPEC.md task #5 log): live devmem read of
# apps_smmu's SCR0 (0x15000000) showed USFCFG=1 -- unidentified/
# unmatched IOMMU streams FAULT by default on this hardware, not
# bypass. The display's real stream ID (mdss's iommus property,
# 0x1c00/mask 0x2 in sm8550.dtsi) has a firmware-provisioned SMR entry
# at a fixed slot (SMR[3] = 0x80021c00, confirmed by scanning all 105
# stream-match groups) -- every raw physical-address SSPP fetch this
# driver ever did was almost certainly silently aborted by the SMMU,
# explaining precisely why frame/line counters increment fine (pure
# INTF timing, no memory access) while zero real pixel content ever
# showed up, with no visible hang. Added smmuBypassMdssStream()
# (forces S2CR[3] to BYPASS before any framebuffer fetch) and
# sheng_mdss_dpu_stop()/sheng_mdss_teardown() (clean pipeline teardown
# before Linux boots, likely fixing the GEM/vmap corruption too).
# Re-enabling to test both fixes together.
CONFIG_VIDEO=y
CONFIG_VIDEO_SHENG_MDSS=y
# Root cause of the CONFIG_VIDEO hang (see git history / SPEC.md):
# growing the u-boot binary enough to cross ~1MB loaded size (physical
# kernel_offset 0x8000 + kernel_size) apparently collides with
# something ABL still has resident there while jumping into "the
# kernel" (actually u-boot.bin) -- confirmed via breadcrumb
# instrumentation that _main's first instruction never even executes
# once that boundary is crossed. Not a bug in any of our code; a size
# budget. Trim what we don't need: the embedded splash logo and its
# BMP decode support save real space for zero functional loss (we
# don't use splash screens).
# CONFIG_VIDEO_LOGO must stay OFF: show_splash() is called from
# video_post_probe() and its failure return propagates out, which would
# abort the console bind that happens after it. The logo is a BMP and
# CONFIG_BMP / CONFIG_VIDEO_BMP_RLE8 are both disabled here.
# CONFIG_VIDEO_LOGO is not set
# CONFIG_VIDEO_BMP_RLE8 is not set
# CONFIG_BMP is not set
# CONFIG_CMD_BMP is not set
# CONFIG_VIDEO pulls these in via `imply`, and CONSOLE_MUX +
# SYS_CONSOLE_IS_IN_ENV means console_init_r() looks up stdin/stdout/
# stderr device names from the saved environment -- this board's env
# was never set up with those and hung silently before any console
# output at all when this was left on (confirmed on hardware). Force
# both off; a single fixed serial console is all that's needed here.
# RE-ENABLED (display now works). These were forced off because the board
# "hung silently before any console output at all" -- the comment above
# names the cause: CONSOLE_MUX + SYS_CONSOLE_IS_IN_ENV make console_init_r()
# look up stdin/stdout/stderr from the environment, and this board's env did
# not define them. sheng.env now sets all three explicitly, so that premise
# no longer holds. Both are required for the panel to show U-Boot's console:
# without SYS_CONSOLE_IS_IN_ENV the env's stdout is never consulted, and
# without CONSOLE_MUX stdout can only be a single device, so the
# "serial,vidconsole" setting cannot take effect.
CONFIG_CONSOLE_MUX=y
CONFIG_SYS_CONSOLE_IS_IN_ENV=y
# The panel is 3048x2032, so an 8x16 bitmap font is ~1/380th of the screen
# width: the console renders correctly but is effectively invisible (7 lines
# read as "nothing on screen", 40 lines only showed up as faint glitching).
# CONSOLE_TRUETYPE would give a scalable font but cannot be built here --
# stb_truetype uses floating point and U-Boot's arm64 build is
# -mgeneral-regs-only. 16x32 is the largest bitmap font available, 4x the
# area of 8x16. Select it alone so it is the one actually used.
# CONFIG_VIDEO_FONT_8X16 is not set
CONFIG_VIDEO_FONT_16X32=y
# U-Boot defaults to BLACK text on a WHITE background. That is why the panel
# showed a white screen after video_clear(), and why the console became
# invisible the moment uc_priv->format was set correctly: the foreground
# ended up black on black. White-on-black is both the conventional choice
# and unambiguous to verify on a panel we cannot otherwise inspect.
CONFIG_SYS_WHITE_ON_BLACK=y
# `version` is used by bootcmd to put real U-Boot build info on the panel.
# U-Boot's own banner is printed before the video device probes and is then
# wiped by video_clear(), so it never reaches the screen; re-emitting it from
# bootcmd is the way to actually see it. Without this the command does not
# exist, bootcmd fails at that point, and every && after it is skipped.
CONFIG_CMD_VERSION=y
